--TEST--
`Core\Sse::current` answers the event stream this program is already writing, so an event written through it lands on the same wire in the same order — `rule:concurrency/two-doors-one-isolate`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The handle carries nothing, so `current()` is not a second stream beside the
// one `stream()` answered: both are handles onto the one stream this context is
// writing, and what proves it is that events sent through either arrive
// interleaved rather than in two runs. That is what lets a helper taking a
// `Core\Sse` be written once and called from both doors — door one's isolate
// never held the handle its upgrade left behind, so `current()` is the only way
// it reaches one, and door two is where the claim is assertable at all, no
// `.nvst` case being a request a server is answering.
var $events = Core\Out::capture(fn (): void => {
    var $opened = Core\Sse::stream();
    $opened->send("ready");
    Core\Sse::current()->send("halfway", event: "progress");
    $opened->send("done");
});
echo $events;
echo "--\n";

// Asking twice is asking the same question twice: neither call opens anything,
// so a program that reaches for the handle in two places writes one stream.
var $twice = Core\Out::capture(fn (): void => {
    Core\Sse::stream();
    Core\Sse::current()->send("first");
    Core\Sse::current()->send("second");
});
echo $twice;
echo "--\n";
--EXPECT--
data: ready

event: progress
data: halfway

data: done

--
data: first

data: second

--
